# LINE ENDINGS, DECLARED RATHER THAN LEFT TO EACH MACHINE.
#
# This repo is worked from both macOS and Windows, and GitHub is the only thing between
# them. Left undeclared, a Windows checkout commits CRLF into shell scripts and headers:
# a small addition arrives as a whole-file rewrite git cannot merge, and CI is handed a
# build-win.sh whose shebang ends in a carriage return. Declaring it here fixes it once
# for both platforms.

# Everything authored here is LF on every platform.
*.c        text eol=lf
*.h        text eol=lf
*.cpp      text eol=lf
*.hpp      text eol=lf
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.py       text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf
*.yaml     text eol=lf
*.json     text eol=lf
*.txt      text eol=lf

# ...except the one .txt that is opened in Notepad on Windows. This rule MUST stay below
# the *.txt line above: in .gitattributes the LAST matching rule wins, and when it sat
# above it, git checked the file out with LF and the eol=crlf was silently ignored.
# (Editor.bat needs no rule of its own; the *.bat line further down already does it.)
tools/win/editor-bundle-README.txt  text eol=crlf
*.command  text eol=lf
*.script   text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are CRLF, because that is what they are.
*.bat      text eol=crlf
*.cmd      text eol=crlf

# The 1995 discs and the cartridge are BYTES, not text. Nothing may touch these: the
# briefings, missions and localisations are CRLF in the original data and stay that way.
*.INI      -text
*.BRF      -text
*.GER      -text
*.ENG      -text
*.FRE      -text
*.MIX      -text
*.SHP      -text
*.VQA      -text
*.AUD      -text
*.IMG      -text
*.PAL      -text
*.pack     -text
